the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${V}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{V}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186329705 "We write down $Q$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Q$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[Q\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\,4 & \\,1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,-1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Q}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Q}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186329821 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $\\det(A)\\ne 0$ is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) & =\\det\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =5k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $5k-5\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “$A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has no solution” is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- Statement “The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point” is true. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statements $\\det(A)\\ne 0$ and “The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point” are true."] [17592186329822 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options $\\det(A)\\ne 0$ and “$A$ is invertible” give us true statements:\n\n- *If the unique solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $\\det(A)\\ne 0$ .* $\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- *If the unique solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $A$ is invertible .*$\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}$"] [17592186336026 "The $4$th row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 11 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}-2} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0}\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{41}}+{\\color{red}(-2)}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{42}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{43}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{44}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}(-2)}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{4+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 8\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 8\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186338731 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & {\\color{red}0} & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & {\\color{red}0} & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & {\\color{red}2} & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & {\\color{red}0} & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{12}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{22}}+{\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{32}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{42}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{3+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186338749 "Using cofactor expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}3} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{11}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{12}}+{\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{13}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{14}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{1+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{21}}+{\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{22}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{23}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{2+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot 5=15.\n$$"] [17592186338750 "Using cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{red}4} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0}\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}4}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{41}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{42}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{43}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{44}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}4}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{4+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =-4\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{21}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{22}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{23}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{2+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(9-2) \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=-4\\cdot (-14)=56.\n$$"] [17592186338751 "The $2$nd row of the given matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So, using a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have   \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}-1} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}1} \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{21}}+{\\color{red}(-1)}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{22}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{23}}+{\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{24}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}(-1)}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{2+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}+{\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{2+4}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$  determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}-1} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{11}}+{\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{12}}+{\\color{red}(-1)}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{13}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{1+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}+{\\color{red}(-1)}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{1+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(6-2)-(-6-2) \\\\  & =-8-(-8) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}2} \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{11}}+{\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{12}}+{\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{13}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{1+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}}+{\\color{red}(2)}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{1+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-6-2)+2(0-6) \\\\  & =-(-8)+2\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =8-12 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=-0+(-4)=-4.\n$$"] [17592186338752 "The $4$th row of the given matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So, using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have   \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}3}\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{41}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{42}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{43}}+{\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{44}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{4+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}+{\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{4+4}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$  determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{red}-1} & {\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}(-1)}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{21}}+{\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{22}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{23}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}(-1)}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{2+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}+{\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{2+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2-3)+2(1-3) \\\\  & =-5+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along its $3$rd row:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}1}\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{31}}+{\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{32}}+{\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{33}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{3+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}}+{\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{3+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(4-2)+(-2+1) \\\\  & =-2+(-1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=-2\\cdot (-9)+3\\cdot (-3)=18-9=9.\n$$"] [17592186341850 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y &=0 \\\\ 2x &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186341852 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y &=1 \\\\ x-y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186341853 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x-3y &=0 \\\\ x+y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186342014 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement $\\det(A)\\ne 0$ is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not invertible” is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solution for some $2\\times 1$ column vectors $\\mathbf{b}.$” is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement $\\det(A)\\ne 0$ is true."] [17592186342015 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement $\\det(A)=0$ is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The system $Ax=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution” is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements “The system $Ax=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution”  and “$A$ is invertible” are true."] [17592186342016 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $\\det(A)=0$ is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) & =\\det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem: \n\n- Statement “The lines $2x+4y=0$ and $3x+6y=0$ intersect at exactly one point” is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist” is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements $\\det(A)=0$ and “${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist” are true."] [17592186342017 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $\\det(A)=0$ is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) & =\\det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $2k-6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “The lines $2x+2y=2$ and $3x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points” is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- Statement “$A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$” is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statement “$A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$” is true."] [17592186342018 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $\\det(A)\\ne 0$ is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) & =\\det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k-8\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 8.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has infinitely many solutions” is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The lines $x+2y=2$ and $4x+ky=0$ don't intersect” is false.  According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement $\\det(A)\\ne 0$ is true."] [17592186342026 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements. \n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis invertible then $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$    \n\nAdditionally, the unique solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the two lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2.$ \n\nFinally, notice that if $k=-1$ then \n\n$$\n\\det\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}=\\det\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=6\\cdot (-1)-2(-3)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, if $A$ is invertible,  then $k\\ne -1.$\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the system's coefficient matrix is invertible, then the lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$,  intersect at exactly one point.*"] [17592186342029 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $k\\ne 1$, then\n\n$$\n\\det(A)=\\det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}=1-k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the unique solution of  $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point. \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then the system $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}$"] [17592186345085 "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\hspace{2em}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\hspace{2em}{\\color{blue}{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}},\\hspace{2em}{\\color{blue}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2}}.$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186345092 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times } \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."] [17592186345095 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\,{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"."] [17592186345097 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times } \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only\"."] [17592186345106 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\en